course: dictionary
lesson: 7736
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Pulp is a word with several meanings. Read the words around it to find out which one is meant. «Inside each cocoa pod is sweet white pulp.» Pods, beans and sweet all point to the soft, wet, squashed inside of a fruit.
explain: «Paper is soaked, mashed into pulp and pressed into new paper.» The clues are soaked, mashed and new paper, so this pulp is the mush of wood or old paper mixed with water. Ask: what would fit here? Then try your meaning in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: The same word can change its job too. As a noun, pulp is a thing. As a verb, to pulp something means to crush it into a soft mass. Pulp stories are a third meaning: cheap, exciting ones that are not very serious.
fact: Pulp is a noun, a verb and an adjective, and each one has its own meaning.
fact: In paper-making, pulp is wood or old paper mashed with water, like «a mushy pulp».
fact: The pulp of a tooth is the soft middle, with the nerve and blood inside.
fact: «Pulp» stories and magazines are cheap, exciting and not very serious.
fact: Orange juice can be «with pulp» or «smooth», so pulp can also mean the soft bits of fruit.
exercise: «Inside each cocoa pod sits sweet white pulp.» What is the pulp here? | A cheap, exciting story | The nerve inside a tooth | *The soft, wet inside of a fruit | The hard shell of a nut
exercise: «Paper is soaked, mashed into pulp and pressed into new paper.» What is the pulp here? | *The mush of wood or old paper mixed with water | A cheap magazine | Hard, dry bark | The soft middle of a tooth
exercise: What does it mean to pulp something? | To lift it up high | *To crush it into a soft mass | To wrap it in paper | To sing it loudly
test: «Under the enamel is dentine, full of tiny tubes leading towards the pulp, the soft middle with the nerve.» What is the pulp? | Juice from a cocoa pod | *The soft middle of a tooth, with the nerve | A cheap, exciting story | Hard white enamel on the outside
test: Why does «Paper is soaked, mashed into pulp» NOT mean fruit? | Fruit is never mashed | The sentence is about a tooth | The word pulp only ever means paper | *The clues soaked, mashed and new paper point to paper-making
exam: «Pulp» stories are cheap and exciting, so what does pulp mean in «pulp stories»? | A soft, squashed mass of fruit | *Cheap, exciting and not very serious | The nerve inside a tooth | Mashed wood for making new paper
